<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Ancient Near East</h1><p class="path-tagline">From the city-states of Sumer to Persia: two thousand years of Near Eastern kingdoms and empires.</p><p class="path-intro">Some of the oldest known law codes and what is widely regarded as the first empire arose in Mesopotamia and the lands around it, from southwestern Iran to Anatolia and the Lebanese coast. The path starts in Lagash, a Sumerian city-state, and follows the powers that rose over the region in turn: Akkad, the Third Dynasty of Ur, Elam, Babylon, the Hittites and Assyria. Urartu in the mountains and the Phoenician cities on the coast lived beside them, and the Achaemenid Persians, who entered Babylon in 539 BCE, gathered most of these lands into one empire.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/lagash"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gudea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 2500 BCE – 2110 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Lagash</span><span class="path-step-why">A Sumerian city-state whose Stele of the Vultures records Eannatum's victory over Umma around 2450 BCE, and whose ruler Urukagina made reforms that may be the first legal code.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Lagash was an ancient Sumerian city-state in southern Mesopotamia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/akkadian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victory-stele-of-naram-sin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 2334 BCE – 2154 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Akkadian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Sargon of Akkad brought the city-states of Sumer and Akkad under one ruler around 2334 BCE, in what is widely regarded as the world's first empire.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Akkadian Empire was the first known empire in Mesopotamia, founded by Sargon of Akkad around the twenty-fourth century BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 2112 BCE – 2004 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Neo-Sumerian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">After the Akkadian collapse the Third Dynasty of Ur revived Sumerian rule, kept tens of thousands of tablets and issued the oldest law code that survives.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Neo-Sumerian Empire, also known as the Third Dynasty of Ur, was a Mesopotamian state that emerged around 2112 BCE after the collapse of the Akkadian Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/elamite-confederation"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 2700 BCE – 539 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Elamite Confederation</span><span class="path-step-why">From Susa and Anshan in southwestern Iran, Elam sacked Ur around 2004 BCE, carried off its last king, Ibbi-Sin, and later overthrew the Kassite dynasty of Babylon.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Elamite Confederation refers to the states of ancient Elam in south-western Iran, which endured for more than two thousand years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/babylonian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1894 BCE – 1595 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Babylonian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Under Hammurabi a small Amorite city-state mastered Mesopotamia in barely a decade, and his Code of 282 laws is the fullest to survive from the ancient Near East.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Old Babylonian Empire, or First Dynasty of Babylon, was an Amorite state in central Mesopotamia that grew to dominate the region under Hammurabi in the eighteenth century BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/hittite-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 1650 BCE – 1178 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hittite Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ruled from Hattusa in Anatolia, the Hittites raided Babylon around 1595 BCE, fought Egypt at Kadesh in 1274 BCE and later concluded a peace treaty with Ramesses II.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Hittite Empire was a major Bronze Age state centred on Hattusa in central Anatolia, at its height in the fourteenth and thirteenth centuries BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/assyrian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nimrud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 2025 BCE – 609 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Assyrian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From the city of Assur, Assyria grew into the largest empire yet seen, reaching Egypt in 671 BCE, until Medes and Babylonians destroyed Nineveh in 612 BCE.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Assyrian Empire was a Mesopotamian state centred on Assur and Nineveh on the upper Tigris.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/van-fortress.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 860 BCE – 590 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Urartu</span><span class="path-step-why">Assyria's great northern rival, a mountain kingdom around Lake Van whose kings built rock fortresses and canals; Argishti I founded Erebuni, the origin of Yerevan, in 782 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/phoenicians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tennes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 1200 BCE – 332 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Phoenicians</span><span class="path-step-why">Rival city-states of the Lebanese coast such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos, which sold cedar and purple cloth and whose ships formed the core of the Persian navy after 539 BCE.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Phoenicians were a Semitic-speaking seafaring people of the Levantine coast, based in city-states such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 559 BCE – 330 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Achaemenid Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Cyrus the Great entered Babylon in 539 BCE, and the empire he built stretched from the Indus to the Aegean and the Nile until Alexander the Great conquered it.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Achaemenid Empire was the first Persian Empire, founded by Cyrus the Great in the sixth century BCE and stretching at its height from the Aegean to the Indus.</span></span></a></li></ol>
